A||||6||Bruce||Distributor O-ring||||1||
Z||000000||Bruce||12-12-2000||01:08 PM||||Where do I get a new distributor O-ring, and how hard is it to replace? I have the small oil drip that other forum members describe as a bad o-ring.<BR>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnnyK||12-12-2000||02:02 PM||||You go to carquest, and it costs 37 cents... How hard is it? You take the distributor out, and pull the old one off and roll the new one on. It's pretty hard jamming the dist. back in there after. Then you have to set timing (well, I did anyways).||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||12-12-2000||03:43 PM||||I would get one from a dealer.  Its different then the aftermarket ones.  The one from the dealer is brown, I believe the Fiero Store carries a dealer one also if that helps.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000003||afRaceR||12-12-2000||05:56 PM||||Bruce....just to save you some time when you go back with the distributor.....before you pull it out, scribe a line on the distributor and the block where they meet and also scribe a line on the top of the distributor where the rotor points. This will set the timing exactly where it was before you disassembled it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||12-12-2000||06:06 PM||||True enough! Remember exactly where the rotor and the dist are pointing..||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mark||12-12-2000||06:18 PM||||It's a standard-size O-ring.  I got it at Pep-Boys.  Less than a buck.<P>Also, when you pull the distributor out, the rotor will turn as the distributor is lifted.  Make yet another mark to indicate where the rotor is pointing when it stops turning.  When reinstalling the distributor, align the rotor with this mark before you install it. This helps get the right gear tooth engagement so that the rotor will point in the correct direction when it is fully inserted.<P>Put some oil on the new O-ring before you install will ease distributor installation.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fiero_Freak||12-13-2000||02:54 AM||||$.22 for the o-ring.  Just make sure to lube it with oil or a bit of hi-temp grease.  And it's definately harder to push back in.  Do scribe that line to where the rotor lines up. And if you pull the distributer, why not change the cap and rotor, $20 for the set, and set the timing.  Who knows how accurate it was before.||||reg||1
